"""Discussion and research lessons; all writes remain under learning."""
from course_writer import finish, lesson, tables, OLD

name = '10_results_and_discussion.md'
finish(name, '''# 10 — What can we honestly conclude?

## Learning objectives

By the end, you should be able to explain the results in everyday language, separate a measured difference from its possible causes, and recommend a use that the evidence can support. Read [evaluation](05_evaluation_and_validation.md) and [explanation experiments](09_explainability_experiments.md) first.

Think of this chapter as a review meeting. Someone asks, “Which model should we trust, and why?” A good answer brings together several kinds of evidence rather than pointing to the highest number.''', [
lesson('A higher score tells us what happened, not why',
'We expected financial pretraining to help. General BERT nevertheless had the higher average macro-F1. How do we explain that without making up a story?',
'First describe the score difference. Then separate possible explanations from explanations that an experiment has actually tested. An unexpected result is still useful evidence.',
'**Measured:** general BERT scored 0.8476 mean macro-F1; our fine-tuned FinBERT scored 0.8339. **Teaching analogy:** if two runners finish at different times, the stopwatch tells us the gap. It does not tell us whether shoes, training or weather caused it.',
'1. Confirm both workflows used the same frozen test rows and label mapping.\n2. Check that their settings were selected with validation data.\n3. Read all three seed results, not just the best run.\n4. State the observed gap.\n5. List possible causes as questions for future experiments.',
'An **observed difference** is a measured comparison. A **causal explanation** identifies what produced that difference. A **controlled experiment** changes the suspected cause while keeping relevant other conditions comparable.',
'The rounded gap is 0.8476 − 0.8339 = **0.0137 macro-F1**, or 1.37 percentage points on the 0–1 scale. That subtraction is not a significance test. Seed SD describes variation among fits on the same dataset; it does not cover every source of uncertainty.',
'The [saved comparison](../artifacts/phase2/comparison/comparison.json) and [training configuration](../configs/phase2.json) establish the observed result. BERT had SD 0.0034; FinBERT had SD 0.0093. Only two learning rates and two epoch budgets were searched, at seed 42. Different pretraining histories and tokenizers remain possible influences. The study did not isolate their separate effects.',
'A wider equal-budget validation search would test whether tuning matters. Matched continued-pretraining experiments could test domain adaptation. More seeds would better describe training variation. Each answers a different question; none should use the already inspected test set to select its winner.',
'Can we say financial pretraining made the model worse?',
'No. We can say this financial-model workflow had a lower measured mean under this protocol. A claim about pretraining itself needs a more controlled comparison.',
'**Common confusion:** a plausible story is not a measured mechanism. “FinBERT overfits filings” is too strong: forgetting, overfitting and genre mismatch were not directly measured here. See the competing tests in [Chapter 11](11_improvements_and_future_research.md).'),
lesson('Reliability is more than one leaderboard number',
'A reviewer sees good overall F1 but asks whether the model struggles on ambiguous news and whether its confidence can be trusted.',
'Think of a model as having several report cards. One grades its class choices. Another grades its confidence. A third checks which kinds of sentences are difficult. Good performance on one does not erase problems on another.',
'**Measured:** BERT allagree macro-F1 is 0.9583, versus 0.7500 for non-allagree. In ordinary language, its performance is much stronger on sentences where the original annotators unanimously agreed. **Toy:** a model can choose the right class on 8 of 10 sentences yet claim 99% confidence on all 10; its confidence is too high.',
'1. Read the overall F1 and class-specific recall.\n2. Split evaluation by agreement membership and inspect class counts.\n3. Compare confidence with correctness using calibration metrics.\n4. Examine edited sentences and selected errors.\n5. Keep each conclusion attached to its evaluation population.',
'A **subgroup** is a stated subset of examples. **Calibration** asks whether stated probabilities match observed frequencies. A **robustness test** checks behavior under a specified change; it does not automatically establish performance on every real-world change.',
'The rounded allagree gap is 0.9583 − 0.7500 = **0.2083**. This is a difference between two observed groups, not the effect of making people agree. Their class mixes differ: the 338 allagree rows contain 50/209/79 negative/neutral/positive examples; the 389 other rows contain 41/223/125.',
'[Phase 3 summary](../artifacts/phase3/summary.json) contains the subgroup, calibration and edit results. FinBERT has lower mean ECE-10 (0.0923 versus 0.1064), while BERT has lower mean Brier and NLL. The metrics ask different questions. Numerical-edit flip rates are low, but doubling a currency amount is not a full test of understanding financial growth.',
'Evaluate class-conditional results, review naturally occurring errors, and create human-checked contrast pairs such as “losses increased” versus “losses decreased.” These give more focused evidence than treating every stable prediction as correct.',
'If a prediction never changes after changing a number, does the model understand numbers?',
'No. The edit may preserve the sentiment, the model may ignore the number, or the changed token may be invisible to its representation. We need validated expected labels and visibility checks to distinguish those cases.',
'**Common confusion:** the 20 annotated errors are selected examples, not a random sample of every mistake. User categories include 7 entity-context cases and only 1 numerical-reasoning case. Their interpretation/comments are labeled AI-assisted notes, not independent human assessments. “Most errors are numerical” is not supported by that file.'),
lesson('Explanation results are useful even without a winning method',
'A stakeholder asks whether IG is a better explanation than attention. Our experiment gives a qualified answer rather than a universal ranking.',
'Two highlighting methods can disagree because they measure different things. To learn more, we remove their highly ranked tokens and watch the original prediction. Even then, the answer depends on how we remove tokens and what we measure.',
'**Measured:** mean per-sentence Spearman correlation is 0.2406 on the 687 numerically converged cases. IG deletion AUC is 0.6555, attention 0.6608 and random 0.7866. Lower AUC means the original predicted-class probability fell more over this masking experiment.',
'1. Keep the fixed FinBERT seed-42 checkpoint and original target class.\n2. Compare rankings on the same content tokens.\n3. Mask increasing fractions and measure probability curves.\n4. Compare methods within each sentence.\n5. Read the uncertainty interval and the 40-case sensitivity analysis before making a claim.',
'An **operational test** defines a concrete measurement for a broader idea. MASK deletion is an operational test of whether ranked tokens support a prediction under that intervention. It is not a complete definition of explanation correctness.',
'IG minus attention mean AUC is about −0.0053. Its paired 95% interval is **[−0.0130, 0.0024]**. Negative values favor IG under this metric, positive values favor attention, and zero means equal mean AUC. Because the interval spans zero, the experiment does not clearly separate the methods.',
'[Phase 5 summary](../artifacts/phase5/full/summary.json) and [Phase 6 sensitivity](../artifacts/phase6/audit-final/ig_sensitivity.json) show the result persists qualitatively when all 727 records are included with warnings: the interval still spans zero. The 40 flagged IG cases are not a random subset. PAD-versus-MASK reference comparisons also changed rankings substantially in the small Phase 4 sample.',
'Whole-word deletion, natural contrast pairs, multiple references and model-randomization checks would broaden the evidence. Each introduces its own assumptions and cost. Agreement with a human highlight measures whether an explanation looks reasonable, which is not identical to whether it reflects model computation.',
'Does an interval spanning zero prove IG and attention are equivalent?',
'No. It means this study does not clearly establish a directional difference. Showing practical equivalence would require an acceptable difference chosen before looking at the results and a suitable analysis.',
'**Common confusion:** passing IG completeness checks numerical accounting along a chosen path. It does not certify a faithful causal story about real language. Replacing many words with MASK creates unusual input; that distribution shift limits the deletion interpretation.'),
lesson('Turn evidence into a bounded deployment recommendation',
'An engineer asks whether the saved checkpoint is ready for automated financial decisions.',
'A successful research evaluation is one part of a deployment decision. We also need to know the real inputs, the cost of errors, who reviews uncertain cases, and whether the data and weights can be used lawfully.',
'**Proposed use:** an analyst receives suggested sentiment labels and reviews the text. **Unsupported use:** automatically buy or sell an asset because a sentence was labeled positive. The project learned sentence sentiment, not future returns.',
'1. State the intended task: short English financial-sentence sentiment.\n2. State observed strengths and weak groups.\n3. Restrict use to research or a monitored human-review pilot.\n4. Check rights, new-data performance, latency and review capacity.\n5. Set acceptance criteria before any wider deployment.',
'**External validity** asks whether findings transfer to a different setting. **Operational validation** asks whether the complete service meets practical requirements. **Provenance** records where data, models and results came from.',
'A simple **toy** service cost illustrates the missing evidence: if 100 missed negative sentences cost 5 review units each, their cost is 500 units. Another system with slightly lower accuracy could be preferable if it avoids those misses. The project did not estimate such real decision costs.',
'The [model card](../publication/MODEL_CARD.md) and [final validation report](../artifacts/phase6/MODEL_VALIDATION_REPORT.md) support a cautious recommendation. [Phase 6 audit code](../src/finbert_explainability/phase6/audit.py) checked 30 manifests and 3025 entries; 3212 earlier files remained byte-identical. The authorized annotation edit remains a disclosed historical manifest exception. A clean audit establishes traceability, not production certification.',
'For a future pilot, compare a CPU baseline, a single encoder and an abstaining workflow that sends uncertain cases to review. Measure latency, class-specific errors, confidence, coverage and reviewer workload. These serving features are proposals, not completed capabilities.',
'Can a frozen test still be unsuitable for choosing the next improvement?',
'Yes. Its bytes can remain unchanged while repeated inspection influences research choices. Future improvement claims need a new independent holdout.',
'**Common confusion:** training time is not serving latency. Unknown upstream overlap, only three training seeds, one explanation checkpoint, small subgroups and narrow perturbations all limit generalization. No serving-latency distribution or rights-cleared production system was established.')],
'## Keep the report cards together\n\nRead each row as a separate question; do not average these different metrics into one invented trust score.\n\n'+tables(name)[0]+'''

The independent lexical baseline has macro-F1 0.6713 and takes only a few seconds for its workflow. ProsusAI scores 0.8797 but remains a **non-independent reference**, because its prior training may overlap PhraseBank. The main explanation checkpoint has 623/727 correct predictions; that single-seed count is not the three-seed mean accuracy.

**Check:** Which result should lead the conclusion? **Answer:** The measured independent comparison, followed immediately by its scope and reliability limitations—not a claim that one model is universally best.''',
[('Why did BERT win?','Its measured mean was higher; the cause has not been isolated.'),('Can lower ECE coexist with worse F1?','Yes. Confidence quality and class decisions are different questions.'),('What does the selected error review establish?','It records categories in 20 chosen errors, not population error frequencies or proven mechanisms.'),('What does the explanation comparison establish?','Modest ranking agreement and better mean masking suppression than random under this protocol; no clear IG-over-attention advantage.'),('What is the defensible recommendation?','Research or a carefully monitored analyst-review pilot, with rights and new-data validation before consequential use.')])

name='11_improvements_and_future_research.md'
lessons=[lesson('Make the next improvement claim testable',
'We have many ideas for improving FinBERT. How do we avoid trying them all and reporting only the one that looks good?',
'Decide what improvement means before looking at new results. Choose a comparison, a meaningful target and safeguards. Keep fresh examples out of development so the final check can still surprise you.',
'**Toy proposal:** compare standard training with a lower learning rate using the same rows and seeds. Decide beforehand that an F1 gain is useful only if negative recall does not fall by an unacceptable amount. The actual acceptable margins must come from the intended use, not this toy example.',
'1. Write a hypothesis that could be wrong.\n2. Identify the single change being tested.\n3. Choose training/validation data and matched compute budgets.\n4. Lock success criteria.\n5. Evaluate a selected workflow on a new independent holdout once.',
'A **hypothesis** is a proposed explanation or prediction. An **ablation** changes or removes one component to learn its contribution. A **holdout** is data reserved for evaluation; it must not guide the choices it is meant to assess.',
'Let M_new and M_control be the same metric on the same new examples. The improvement is Δ = M_new − M_control. In a **toy** result, 0.85 − 0.83 = 0.02. A positive difference alone does not show that it is stable or useful; compare paired uncertainty with a practical margin chosen beforehand.',
'The current 727 test sentences have repeatedly informed analysis. Keep them as a historical benchmark, but use a new time/event-separated, deduplicated holdout for meaningful future claims. Start from [Phase 2 settings](../configs/phase2.json), [saved selection](../artifacts/phase2/finbert/selection.json) and [training code](../src/finbert_explainability/phase2/neural.py). No proposal in this chapter has been run for the course.',
'A broad model sweep explores more options but can overfit the validation set and obscure causes. A narrow ablation is easier to interpret but may miss interactions. A staged program diagnoses first, tests a few changes second, and confirms independently last.',
'Is a new random split of the already inspected sentences a new independent dataset?',
'No. The content has already influenced development. It can help internal diagnostics, but it cannot provide the same protection as newly collected, independently evaluated data.',
'**Common confusion:** locking the final file is not enough if its content already shaped the hypothesis. Priority labels below are planning judgments, not predicted or measured gains.'),
lesson('Compare competing explanations for BERT’s advantage',
'The observed gap is small enough that several mechanisms could matter. Choosing a favorite story too soon can lead to an expensive but uninformative intervention.',
'Treat each explanation as a suspect. Ask what it predicts, what evidence we already have, and what test would make it less believable. Several suspects may contribute at once.',
'**Measured:** FinBERT seed SD is 0.0093 versus BERT 0.0034. **Hypothesis:** optimization is less stable for the financial checkpoint. **Counterpoint:** three runs are too few to identify a mechanism, and different validation-selected checkpoints can occur without harmful instability.',
'1. State the mechanism in plain language.\n2. Find existing supporting observations without turning them into proof.\n3. Identify missing measurements.\n4. Design a comparison that holds other changes fixed.\n5. Specify what result would weaken the hypothesis.',
'A **confounding difference** is another change that could explain the result. **Catastrophic forgetting** means new training damages useful knowledge acquired earlier. A **learning curve** tracks performance as training data or training time changes.',
'In a **toy** paired study, adaptation helps by +0.02 on one new sample and −0.01 on another. Their average is +0.005, but the disagreement matters. Repeated datasets and paired seeds help distinguish a dependable effect from a setting-specific gain; no such adaptation result was measured here.',
'The evidence motivating the hypothesis cards below is concrete: 3391 training rows; a four-candidate single-search-seed grid; FinBERT seed 123 selecting epoch 1 while the other two select epoch 4; different vocabularies; and large agreement-subgroup gaps. These observations motivate tests but do not determine why BERT scored higher.',
'More seeds are cheap compared with pretraining from scratch but cannot isolate corpus causes. Controlled continued pretraining is more feasible than recreating entire historical model training, yet it tests a new adaptation intervention, not the full history of the two existing checkpoints.',
'Would swapping FinBERT’s tokenizer for BERT’s isolate vocabulary effects?',
'No. Token IDs would point to different embedding rows, breaking the learned correspondence unless embeddings and training were handled appropriately. Begin with descriptive tokenization analysis on development data.',
'**Common confusion:** “finance” is not one uniform text style. Reports, calls and short news differ. That makes genre mismatch plausible, not established. The detailed cards preserve nine competing hypotheses below.'),
lesson('Change the loss carefully: difficult does not always mean useful',
'Negative examples are less common, and some labels are ambiguous. Should training pay extra attention to difficult or minority examples?',
'A training loss is the penalty used to update weights. We can increase the penalty for a minority class, reduce the influence of easy examples, or avoid pretending every label is perfectly certain. Each changes what the model is encouraged to learn.',
'**Toy:** the correct class has probability 0.8. Ordinary cross-entropy is −ln(0.8) ≈ 0.2231. A class weight of 2 makes it 0.4463. Focal loss with exponent 2 multiplies by (1−0.8)² = 0.04, giving about 0.0089 without class weighting. An easy example receives much less pressure.',
'1. Start with standard cross-entropy as the control.\n2. Estimate any class weights from training labels only.\n3. Tune one change using validation data.\n4. Inspect class precision/recall and confidence, not only F1.\n5. Check whether difficult examples were useful cases or noisy labels.',
'**Class weighting** gives different labels different loss weights. **Focal loss** reduces easy-example contributions. **Label smoothing** replaces a perfectly certain training target with a slightly softer one. **Soft labels** are probability targets, ideally based on actual votes rather than invented uncertainty.',
r'''For true class y, predicted probability p_y, class weight w_y and focal exponent γ, weighted loss is −w_y ln(p_y); focal loss is −w_y(1−p_y)^γ ln(p_y). Setting γ=0 gives weighted cross-entropy.

For K=3 classes and smoothing ε=0.1, q_k=(1−ε)1[k=y]+ε/K. The true-class target becomes 0.9333 and the other two become 0.0333 each. Here 1[k=y] is 1 for the true class and 0 otherwise. The soft-target loss is −Σ_k q_k ln(p_k). This is a proposed rule, not recovered annotator votes.''',
'The completed project used ordinary supervised loss, not these proposed alternatives. Agreement bands do not reveal the three class vote proportions. [Phase 3 subgroup results](../artifacts/phase3/summary.json) motivate careful testing; they do not justify discarding all non-unanimous rows.',
'Class weighting is simple and cheap but may hurt confidence calibration. Focal loss may concentrate on mislabeled examples. Smoothing may reduce overconfidence but blur genuinely clear labels. True soft labels require additional annotation evidence.',
'Can a 25–34% disagreement band directly become a three-class soft label?',
'No. It constrains overall agreement but does not say how the remaining votes divide between the other classes, nor give exact vote totals.',
'**Common confusion:** improving minority recall can lower precision or accuracy. An intervention is not a success just because the metric it directly encourages improves. Evaluate classwise safeguards and probability quality on independent data.'),
lesson('Adapt knowledge, combine predictions, or reduce model cost',
'If a bigger training search is insufficient, should we add domain text, combine models, or build a smaller model?',
'These solve different problems. Extra unlabeled reading may improve representations. Combining predictions may average away some mistakes. A smaller student may copy useful behavior at lower serving cost. None guarantees better sentiment decisions.',
'**Toy ensemble:** two models assign positive probability 0.9 and 0.5. Their equally weighted average is 0.7. That is a new prediction distribution, not the average of their eventual F1 scores. **Toy distillation:** a teacher’s [0.1,0.2,0.7] target tells a student more than the hard label “positive” alone.',
'1. Choose the bottleneck: language coverage, prediction quality or serving cost.\n2. Pick the matching intervention.\n3. Keep lawful training-side data separate from evaluation.\n4. Include an unchanged-model control.\n5. Measure the intended benefit and the possible loss of other skills.',
'**Domain-adaptive pretraining (DAPT)** continues language-model training on domain text. **Task-adaptive pretraining (TAPT)** uses unlabeled text close to the task. Both are forms of continued pretraining. **Ensembling** combines model predictions. **Knowledge distillation** trains a student to imitate a teacher. **Multi-task learning** shares training across related labeled tasks.',
r'''An equal ensemble uses p_ensemble,k = (1/S)Σ_s p_s,k, where S is the number of models and k is a class. The probabilities still sum to 1.

One proposed distillation loss combines ordinary label loss with T² KL(p_teacher^(T) || p_student^(T)). KL(q||p)=Σ_k q_k ln(q_k/p_k) is zero for identical distributions and penalizes mismatch. T is a temperature that softens probabilities; the mixture weight and T must be selected on development data. This formula explains a candidate method, not an implemented training path.''',
'The existing models were fine-tuned from saved upstream checkpoints; the project did not run DAPT, TAPT, distillation, ensembling or multi-task training. A financial-news adaptation experiment could test genre mismatch. A student or cascade would address deployment cost, which was not measured by the training-duration table.',
'Adaptation costs GPU time and risks contamination or forgetting. Ensembles multiply inference/storage and can share the same errors. Distillation adds teacher inference and student training, and can copy teacher biases. Frozen sentence embeddings with a linear head are cheaper to fit but may miss subtle task-specific distinctions.',
'Does an ensemble of three seeds necessarily score the mean seed F1?',
'No. Average the probability vectors, choose classes, then recompute F1. F1 is nonlinear, so averaging member scores answers a different question.',
'**Common confusion:** an LLM’s fluent explanation is generated text, not proof of the computation behind its label. Any LLM comparison needs fixed prompts, label parsing, model versions, data-handling rules and contamination checks.'),
lesson('Prioritize evidence before expensive training',
'An RTX 4070 laptop can support useful controlled experiments, but a long list of ideas can exhaust time without answering a clear question.',
'Start with inexpensive measurements that can rule out bad ideas. Secure an independent test first. Then spend compute where the existing evidence points, with a clear stopping rule.',
'**Measured motivation:** 15 test inputs were truncated, so longer context is worth investigating on development examples. It does not follow that doubling the sequence length will improve the whole benchmark. **Toy cost:** dense attention pairs grow from 64²=4096 to 128²=16384, four times as many pairs per head.',
'1. Establish new data provenance and label rules.\n2. Run development-only diagnostics for tokenization, learning curves and calibration.\n3. Test a small number of matched interventions.\n4. Confirm selected workflows on a locked holdout.\n5. Report failures and compute, not only favorable results.',
'A **safeguard** is a requirement that must remain acceptable while optimizing another metric. A **cost–quality frontier** compares systems where improving quality tends to require more compute or latency. **p95 latency** is the response time below which 95% of measured requests fall.',
'A **toy** review policy sends 30 of 100 inputs to a human, so automatic coverage is 70/100 = 70%. Its automatic error rate must be computed on those 70 accepted cases. A lower accepted-case error rate is not meaningful without also reporting coverage and reviewer workload.',
'The roadmap below uses FinBERT non-allagree F1 0.7262, ECE-10 seed SD 0.0556, and explanation numerical/baseline issues as motivation. These are different reliability problems; one new training trick need not solve all of them. The [Phase 6 sensitivity](../artifacts/phase6/audit-final/ig_sensitivity.json) must remain a frozen historical control.',
'P0 establishes valid evaluation. P1 favors directly motivated, relatively inexpensive work. P2 needs more engineering or annotation. P3 is expensive or weakly supported by current evidence. These are proposed priorities, not measured return-on-investment forecasts.',
'Should we optimize IG–attention correlation to make explanations better?',
'Not by itself. Two explanations agreeing more could simply share the same mistake. Evaluate sensitivity, numerical reliability and behavior under justified interventions instead.',
'**Common confusion:** selecting the intervention or equivalence margin after seeing a holdout result weakens the claim. Keep all new configurations/results in new locations; never overwrite this project’s evidence.')]

# Convert dense comparison tables into individually readable evidence cards.
ts=tables(name)
def rows(table):
    return [[x.strip() for x in line.strip('|').split('|')] for line in table.splitlines()[2:]]
cards=['## Research notebook A: nine explanations to test\n\nAll cards are hypotheses, not findings. Read one at a time after Lesson 2. For each, ask what result would make you abandon it.']
examples=[
('Imagine two training shuffles emphasizing different rare negative examples.','Would more seeds alone test corpus mismatch?','No. They characterize training variation while leaving the corpus difference intact.'),
('Compare “net proceeds pursuant to the offering” with “profits jump.” Both are financial language, but their style differs.','Does a financial label guarantee the same language distribution?','No. Genre and task can differ within one domain.'),
('Count how many pieces each tokenizer produces for the same company name before changing either model.','Is fragmentation automatically harmful?','No. It is a diagnostic; downstream comparisons are needed.'),
('Train from one shared base on equal amounts of general and financial text.','Would that reproduce both historical models?','No. It tests a controlled adaptation effect, not their entire histories.'),
('A four-point search might miss a useful lower learning rate.','May we enlarge only FinBERT’s search and call the comparison equal-budget?','No. Account for and match the resources or state the different question.'),
('A high first update can move many pretrained weights before the classifier becomes useful.','Does selecting epoch 1 prove instability?','No. It motivates examining curves and controlled optimization changes.'),
('Check a separate language task before and after sentiment training.','Is lower sentiment accuracy sufficient evidence of forgetting?','No. We must measure loss of previously demonstrated capability.'),
('Two annotators can reasonably disagree about “revenue rose but margins fell.”','Would deleting difficult labels solve the deployment task?','It changes the population and may hide the hard cases users still encounter.'),
('Compare “A acquired B” with “B acquired A” while holding the names fixed.','Do shared words guarantee shared meaning?','No. Relations and syntax change who did what.')]
for i,(r,e) in enumerate(zip(rows(ts[0]),examples),1):
    cards.append(f'### A{i}. {r[0].split("**")[1]}\n\n**Start with an example:** {e[0]}\n\n**Mechanism:** {r[0]}\n\n**Existing evidence:** {r[1]}\n\n**What we still need:** {r[2]}\n\n**Test step by step:** {r[3]}\n\n**Check:** {e[1]} **Answer:** {e[2]}')
cards.append('## Research notebook B: improvement cards\n\nEach card states its mechanism, cost, risk and proper evaluation. These proposals build on Lessons 3–5; none is an achieved improvement.')
examples2=['Reserve later news by event before developing a new model.','Try different rates with the same total fit budget for both encoders.','Begin updates gently; optionally update lower layers more slowly.','Give a negative training example a larger loss weight.','Reduce the loss pressure from an already easy example.','Use targets 0.9333/0.0333/0.0333 instead of 1/0/0.','Use actual votes such as 6/10 positive, 3/10 neutral and 1/10 negative.','Inspect clear and disputed examples separately before inventing weights.','Continue masked-word learning on lawful financial news.','Continue masked-word learning on training-side task text.','Check whether more recent text changes both useful and harmful associations.','Paraphrase a sentence only when its financial meaning is preserved.','Ask reviewers to label a diverse set of uncertain new sentences.','Train a smaller student using a fixed teacher’s probabilities.','Average probability vectors, then evaluate the resulting classifier.','Train entity relations alongside sentiment with separate ablations.','Fit a temperature on validation logits, leaving predicted classes unchanged.','Teach a reviewed invariance such as a harmless company rename.','Compare 64 and 128 tokens only after checking where evidence was cut off.']
for i,(r,ex) in enumerate(zip(rows(ts[1]),examples2),1):
    cards.append(f'### B{i}. {r[0]}\n\n**Concrete starting point:** {ex} This is a proposal, not a completed experiment.\n\n**Why it could help:** {r[1]}\n\n**Cost and complexity:** {r[2]}\n\n**What can go wrong:** {r[3]}\n\n**Walkthrough for evaluation:** {r[4]}\n\n**Check:** What would make this proposal a defensible improvement? **Answer:** A matched development comparison followed by independent confirmation, with its stated risks measured—not merely a higher score after repeated test inspection.')
cards.append('## Research notebook C: choose a model for the constraint\n\nA frozen embedding classifier turns each sentence into a fixed feature vector and fits a small head. A zero-shot LLM uses instructions without labeled examples in its prompt; few-shot adds examples. A cascade routes only selected inputs to a more expensive component. None has been benchmarked here beyond the completed workflows.\n\n'+ts[2]+'\n\n**Check:** Which family has the best latency? **Answer:** We cannot rank unmeasured systems from architecture names alone. Measure on the intended hardware, input lengths and batch/concurrency settings. The table describes likely cost drivers, not benchmark timings.')
road=OLD[name].split('### D. Prioritized next research experiments\n')[1].split('## 3.')[0].strip()
cards.append('## Research notebook D: a sequence of controlled experiments\n\nRead these as an execution proposal. No training is authorized or performed by this learning material. Each item supplies a hypothesis, controls and acceptance criteria.\n\n'+road+'\n\n**Check:** Why is collecting a holdout first priority even though it does not improve weights? **Answer:** Without an independent check, we cannot tell whether later improvements generalize beyond examples that shaped our choices.')
finish(name,'''# 11 — Design better experiments before promising a better model

## Learning objectives

Explain nine competing reasons for BERT’s higher score, understand candidate improvements from simple examples, compare their costs and risks, and design a research plan that could reject your favorite idea. Read [training](04_baselines_and_training.md), [calibration](06_calibration_and_robustness.md) and [discussion](10_results_and_discussion.md) first.

**Everything proposed here is future work.** The course does not retrain, retune, calibrate or replace a saved model. The historical rationale is not assumed: priorities below are technical judgments based on the evidence.''',lessons,'\n\n'.join(cards),[
('What could weaken the domain-mismatch hypothesis?','Repeated matched financial-news adaptation failing to help while another controlled intervention explains the gap would weaken it; one negative result does not prove impossibility.'),('Why not reconstruct soft labels from agreement bands?','The exact votes across all three classes are missing.'),('Why can focal loss hurt?','Hard examples may be ambiguous or mislabeled, so emphasizing them can amplify noise.'),('What is the first prerequisite for a meaningful improvement claim?','A clearly defined goal and an independent evaluation population that has not shaped the intervention.'),('How do you defend an unexpected result?','State what was measured, show the uncertainty, separate competing mechanisms, and propose a test that could prove your preferred explanation wrong.')])
